S623 UNIT-DISTANCE IMPAIRMENT ATLAS
===================================
INSPIRATION IMPORTED FROM OTHER THREADS
---------------------------------------
- LRC: a quotient is useful only when side channels are retained; impairments test which labels are load-bearing.
- Tournaments: scalar counts miss forbidden packet structure; impairments should target packets, not only totals.
- Cauldrons: hostile turn schedules expose which resource a one-player process forgot to protect.
- Equidecomposability: equal edge counts can be non-equivalent; keep construction class and deletion deck.
- S617 frontier gains: update the local observable state-locally; impaired methods recompute or forget gain shape.

SMALL WIDTH IMPAIRMENT: MOSER CARRIER
-------------------------------------
target=14, policy=healthy; width is the impaired resource.
  width=  1: true_edges=28, span= 6, frontier_gain_hist={1: 175, 2: 9, 3: 1}
  width=  3: true_edges=29, span= 6, frontier_gain_hist={1: 174, 2: 10}
  width= 10: true_edges=29, span= 6, frontier_gain_hist={1: 174, 2: 10}
  width= 30: true_edges=33, span= 4, frontier_gain_hist={1: 111, 2: 34, 3: 1, 4: 1}
  width= 60: true_edges=33, span= 4, frontier_gain_hist={1: 111, 2: 34, 3: 1, 4: 1}
Reading: small widths are a deliberate adversary.  If the best edge count
stabilizes early but the gain histogram does not, the proof object should
retain future-gain shape rather than only current edge count.

